\begin{lemma}[Local-buffer retention]
\label{lem:5-buffer}
Let $V_t$ be the candidates scored by a block through chunk $t$, and
$B_t$ its valid buffer after any compaction or filtering at that point.
Assume fixed complete keys, sufficient append capacity and exact
top-$c$ compaction. Let $\beta_t$ be the minimum of the complete shared
bounds observed so far, including the initial top sentinel. Then
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Top}_c(V_t)\cap\{x:K_q(x)\preceq\beta_t\}
 \subseteq B_t.
 \label{eq:5-buffer-invariant}
\end{equation}
With sharing disabled, the bound is the sentinel and the buffer contains
the whole prefix top-$c$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The empty prefix satisfies the invariant. A new candidate can fail the
local admission threshold only if $c$ previously retained keys precede
it; those keys remain predecessors in every later prefix. Rejection by
an observed shared bound excludes only keys above $\beta_t$. An old
candidate outside a prefix top-$c$ cannot improve its rank when the
prefix grows. Thus every eligible new prefix winner is either retained
already or admitted. Exact compaction preserves it: fewer than $c$
members of the whole prefix, and hence of the buffer, precede it.
Filtering removes only keys above an observed bound. Induction over
appends, compactions and filtering proves the claim. The running minimum
is essential if reads may return different stale published values.
\end{proof}
\end{resultblock}

